      "request_text": "problem_type: prescriptive\n\nobjective: >\n  Determine the most structurally sound architecture for a portable,\n  document-driven runtime that supports:\n    - `pm>` prompt optimization\n    - `tell codex ...` instruction drafting\n    - A `communicate`-based request lifecycle\n  while maximizing determinism, portability, auditability, and long-term extensibility.\n\ndecision_required:\n  Select one architectural model and justify the choice based on:\n    - determinism\n    - idempotence guarantees\n    - enforcement strength\n    - implementation complexity\n    - future extensibility\n    - alignment with DRA rigor\n\narchitectural_options:\n\n  option_A_cli_orchestrated:\n    description: >\n      Deterministic CLI runtime that enforces a document protocol.\n      Codex acts only when invoked explicitly via commands.\n    properties:\n      - Clear state machine enforcement.\n      - Strong idempotence guarantees.\n      - Lower autonomy.\n      - Easier to test.\n      - Higher structural safety.\n    risks:\n      - Less \u201cagent-like\u201d.\n      - Slightly more manual orchestration.\n\n  option_B_single_command_agent:\n    description: >\n      Single keyword (e.g., \"communicate\") triggers full lifecycle execution.\n      Codex scans INPUT PAD, plans, executes, logs, and finalizes autonomously.\n    properties:\n      - High automation.\n      - Feels like a lightweight agent runtime.\n      - Reduced friction for user.\n    risks:\n      - Harder to enforce invariants.\n      - Higher risk of drift or duplicate execution.\n      - More complex failure handling.\n\n  option_C_hybrid_layered:\n    description: >\n      Deterministic CLI core (state machine + protocol enforcement)\n      with a thin agent layer that orchestrates lifecycle steps\n      but must comply with CLI-enforced transitions.\n    properties:\n      - Deterministic enforcement layer.\n      - Agent convenience on top.\n      - Clear separation of protocol vs execution logic.\n      - Scales better.\n    risks:\n      - Slightly more architectural complexity.\n      - Requires clean interface contracts.\n\nevaluation_standard:\n  - Prevents silent drift.\n  - Prevents duplicate execution of DONE items.\n  - Maintains portability across arbitrary repos.\n  - Keeps runtime small and copy/paste friendly.\n  - Supports DRA-level rigor if strict mode enabled.\n\nrequired_output:\n  - Explicit recommendation (A, B, or C).\n  - Technical justification.\n  - Tradeoff explanation.\n  - Suggested next-step implementation direction.\n  - Any required constraint tightening to prevent drift.\n\nconstraints:\n  - No expansion beyond defined runtime concept.\n  - No hidden services.\n  - Preserve copy/paste portability.\n  - Prefer structural correctness over novelty.",
      "codex_ack_plan": "Assess options A/B/C against determinism, idempotence, enforcement strength, complexity, extensibility, and DRA rigor; recommend one model; record tradeoffs and concrete next-step implementation constraints.",
      "execution_log": "- 2026-02-13T00:02:12+00:00 Completed comparative architecture analysis with strict drift-prevention focus.",
      "outputs": "Recommendation: C (hybrid layered). Justification: A provides strongest deterministic enforcement but sacrifices ergonomics; B improves automation but weakens invariant enforcement and increases duplicate/drift risk. C preserves a deterministic CLI/state-machine core for hard guarantees while adding a thin orchestration layer for communicate convenience. This best balances determinism, idempotence, enforcement strength, portability, and extensibility under DRA rigor. Tradeoffs: C is slightly more complex than A due to interface boundary design, but complexity is localized and testable. Next-step direction: Keep communicate_scan.py as sole state authority; add a small orchestrator command that calls core transitions (consume -> ack -> log -> complete/block) and refuses direct state mutation outside CLI APIs. Constraint tightening to prevent drift: enforce terminal-state immutability; require explicit request_id; require monotonic transition checks; add conflict/lock handling for concurrent writes; fail closed on parse errors with BLOCKED status and exact repair guidance.",
      "evidence": "Commands: consume, ack, log, complete via commscribe/scripts/communicate_scan.py; Source analyzed: commscribe/communicate.md INPUT PAD for REQ-20260212-180201.",
      "next_steps_if_blocked": "",
      "objective": "problem_type: prescriptive"
