      "request_id": "REQ-20260212-222611",
      "status": "DONE",
      "author": "USER",
      "created_at": "2026-02-13T04:26:11+00:00",
      "last_updated_at": "2026-02-13T04:27:38+00:00",
      "request_text": "problem_type: prescriptive\n\nobjective: >\n  Instruct Codex to evaluate whether to:\n    (A) perform a large-scale cleanup/refactor within the current repository, or\n    (B) extract clean, stable subsystems into a new repository for restructuring,\n  and to propose a structured, staged plan before making any changes.\n\ndirective_to_codex: |\n  We are considering either:\n    1) Performing a major structural cleanup of this repository in place, or\n    2) Extracting stable components into a new, clean repository and reorganizing there.\n\n  Do not execute changes yet.\n\n  Step 1 \u2014 Recommendation First\n  - Analyze current repository structure.\n  - Identify structural complexity, duplication, drift, or entropy.\n  - Recommend either:\n      A) In-place cleanup\n      B) New repository extraction\n  - Justify the recommendation based on:\n      * risk\n      * scope of change\n      * potential breakage\n      * long-term maintainability\n      * CI/CD complexity\n      * tooling coupling\n\n  Step 2 \u2014 Provide Structured Plan Options\n  For the recommended path (and briefly for the alternative):\n  - Provide a staged migration/cleanup plan.\n  - Define clear boundaries of responsibility.\n  - Specify what should be frozen during transition.\n  - Identify rollback strategy.\n\n  Step 3 \u2014 Consolidation Opportunities\n  Identify opportunities for structural improvement, including but not limited to:\n    - Consolidation of all development_docs into a standardized docs structure.\n    - Standardization of audit output format and naming conventions.\n    - Standardization of test output artifacts.\n    - Clear file naming conventions for REQ blocks, compliance records, and reports.\n    - Removal or archiving of redundant artifacts.\n    - Enforced directory structure for:\n        * runtime\n        * docs\n        * audits\n        * compliance\n        * tests\n        * UI\n        * CI\n\n  Step 4 \u2014 Automation Opportunities\n  Propose automation mechanisms such as:\n    - Auto-generated HTML status/progress dashboard.\n    - Automatic compliance matrix rendering.\n    - Status summary generation from canonical JSON.\n    - Build-time integrity validation.\n    - Periodic artifact cleanup rules.\n  For each suggestion:\n    - Explain benefit.\n    - Explain implementation complexity.\n    - Identify whether it affects runtime invariants.\n\n  Step 5 \u2014 Risk Classification\n  Classify risks for:\n    - Massive in-place cleanup.\n    - Extraction to new repository.\n    - Partial consolidation.\n  Label risks:\n    * Low (cosmetic)\n    * Medium (structural refactor)\n    * High (runtime invariant risk)\n\n  Step 6 \u2014 Decision Output Format\n  Provide:\n    - Clear recommended path (A or B).\n    - Justification (concise).\n    - Phased execution plan.\n    - Optional enhancements list.\n    - Explicit \"Do Not Change\" invariants (runtime core, failure semantics, etc.).\n\nconstraints:\n  - Do not execute changes.\n  - Do not rename directories yet.\n  - Do not delete artifacts.\n  - No runtime behavior modifications.\n  - Preserve DRA-hard guarantees.\n  - Prioritize structural clarity and portability.\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - Clear, risk-aware recommendation.\n  - Efficient migration/cleanup plan.\n  - Explicit protection of runtime invariants.\n  - Concrete automation ideas grounded in current architecture.",
      "codex_ack_plan": "Analyze repository structure and artifact entropy, recommend in-place cleanup vs new-repo extraction, provide staged plan with freeze/rollback/invariants/automation/risk classification without executing changes.",
      "execution_log": "- 2026-02-13T04:26:45+00:00 Collected repository-level structural signals to support migration-path recommendation and staged plan.",
      "outputs": "## Decision\n\nRecommended path: **B) Extract stable subsystems into a new repository for restructuring**.\n\nConcise justification:\n- **Risk**: current repo has high artifact entropy (many top-level operational/audit docs + deep mixed-purpose trees), so large in-place cleanup has higher accidental breakage risk.\n- **Scope control**: extraction lets you define clean boundaries first, then migrate only stable runtime/core pieces.\n- **Breakage containment**: host app remains operational while new repo is hardened independently.\n- **Maintainability**: new repo can enforce a normalized structure from day 1.\n- **CI/CD complexity**: introducing strict gates is simpler in a clean target than retrofitting across mixed legacy outputs.\n- **Tooling coupling**: current project-specific coupling stays isolated while reusable runtime standards mature.\n\n## Compliance-Aware Plan\n\n### Recommended path (B): staged extraction plan\n\n1. Stage 0: Freeze + boundary definition\n- Freeze during transition:\n  - runtime core behavior (`commscribe/scripts/communicate_scan.py`, failure semantics)\n  - request state schema and transition contract\n  - blocking/recovery semantics (`SYSTEM_BLOCKED`/`SYSTEM_RECOVERED`)\n- Define extraction boundary:\n  - include: runtime, tests, core docs/contracts, minimal scripts\n  - exclude: host-specific logs, one-off audits, project-specific artifacts\n\n2. Stage 1: Skeleton target repo creation\n- Create fixed structure in target repo for: `runtime`, `docs`, `audits`, `compliance`, `tests`, `ui`, `ci`.\n- Copy only bounded artifact set.\n\n3. Stage 2: Verification in target repo\n- Run deterministic checklist (healthy no-op, markdown/json corruption+recovery, contention test).\n- Require pass before any refactor.\n\n4. Stage 3: Consolidation and naming normalization\n- Consolidate docs/audits/report naming conventions.\n- Normalize test output locations and artifact retention policy.\n\n5. Stage 4: CI enforcement\n- Add invariant checks + test gates as required checks.\n- Add integrity validation step for canonical files/markers.\n\n6. Stage 5: Optional host back-port\n- Back-port only proven conventions to this host repo in narrow, low-risk increments.\n\nRollback strategy:\n- If target validation fails, stop and revert to previous tag/snapshot in target repo; host repo remains unchanged.\n\n### Alternative path (A): in-place cleanup (brief)\n\n- Execute by strict slices (docs only, then tests, then runtime-adjacent).\n- Requires stronger freeze discipline and more rollback checkpoints.\n- Higher coordination overhead and higher incidental breakage risk due to mixed existing structure.\n\n## Consolidation Opportunities\n\n- Standardize `development_docs` into one canonical docs hierarchy with fixed sections (`decisions`, `audits`, `reports`, `archive`).\n- Standardize audit output naming (`AUDIT_<scope>_<YYYYMMDD>.md`) and report naming (`REPORT_<topic>_<YYYYMMDD>.md`).\n- Standardize test artifacts under one root (`test_results/<suite>/<date>/`).\n- Standardize compliance/request artifacts:\n  - REQ records remain canonical in `communicate.json`/`communicate.md`\n  - compliance snapshots in `commscribe/docs/` with predictable names\n- Archive redundant root-level status docs; keep a single current handoff/status pointer.\n- Enforce directory ownership boundaries:\n  - runtime: `commscribe/scripts`\n  - docs: `docs`, `development_docs` (or extracted equivalent)\n  - audits/compliance: dedicated subtrees\n  - tests/UI/CI separated explicitly\n\n## Automation Opportunities\n\n1. Auto-generated HTML status dashboard\n- Benefit: single operational view of request state and compliance status.\n- Complexity: medium.\n- Invariant impact: low if read-only from canonical JSON.\n\n2. Automatic compliance matrix rendering\n- Benefit: removes manual drift in compliance summaries.\n- Complexity: medium.\n- Invariant impact: low if derived-only output.\n\n3. Status summary generation from canonical JSON\n- Benefit: deterministic concise handoff artifacts.\n- Complexity: low.\n- Invariant impact: none if non-authoritative outputs only.\n\n4. Build-time integrity validation\n- Benefit: catches marker/schema breakage before merge.\n- Complexity: low-medium.\n- Invariant impact: positive (enforcement).\n\n5. Periodic artifact cleanup policy automation (archive/prune rules)\n- Benefit: controls repository entropy.\n- Complexity: medium.\n- Invariant impact: none if it excludes canonical runtime state files.\n\n## Risk Classification\n\n- Massive in-place cleanup: **High** (runtime invariant risk via broad blast radius).\n- Extraction to new repository: **Medium** (migration/coordination risk, lower runtime risk).\n- Partial consolidation in-place: **Medium** (structural refactor risk, controllable by strict slices).\n\n## Do Not Change Invariants\n\n- Scanner remains sole state authority for request lifecycle mutations.\n- Terminal state immutability and monotonic transitions.\n- `SYSTEM_BLOCKED` fail-closed behavior on all entrypaths.\n- Source-aware failure tracking/recovery semantics.\n- Shared lock + atomic write contract for mutating paths.\n- No direct UI rewrite of canonical request sections.\n\n## Final Output\n\n- Recommended path: **B (new repository extraction)**\n- Rationale: lower breakage risk, cleaner CI/enforcement adoption, better long-term portability and maintainability.\n- Execution style: staged, validation-gated, rollback-safe.",
      "evidence": "Repository structural signals used:\n- Top-level file/dir inventory (`ls -1`, `find . -maxdepth 1 -type f`, `find . -maxdepth 2 -type d`).\n- Documentation density sample:\n  - files in development/docs trees: 207\n- Runtime artifact density sample:\n  - files under logs + test_results: 178\n- Root-level status/audit docs observed: HANDOFF.md, LATEST_UPDATE.md, PHASE_D_VERIFICATION.md, SESSION_HANDOFF_2026-02-13.md, TECHNICAL_AUDIT.md.\n\nNo code/runtime changes were executed.",
      "next_steps_if_blocked": "",
      "objective": "problem_type: prescriptive"
    },
