      "request_text": "problem_type: prescriptive\n\nobjective: >\n  Generate a commscribe request proposing the top three lowest\u2013risk cleanup\n  slices for the massive project, based on the existing interactive inventory\n  report findings, without performing any cleanup yet.\n\ncommunicate_request_block_text: |\n  ## REQ: Top 3 Lowest-Risk Cleanup Proposals Based on Inventory\n\n  ### Objective\n  Review the interactive inventory report under `reports/inventory/` and\n  propose three distinct, low\u2013risk cleanup/refactor slices that can\n  improve structural clarity without broad changes or high breakage risk.\n  Do *not* execute any changes in this request; only propose them.\n\n  ### Scope\n  Analyze inventory findings such as:\n    - entropy hotspots\n    - category counts\n    - redundant or derived artifacts\n    - root-level docs\n    - naming inconsistency signals\n\n  For each proposed slice, provide:\n    - Title (clear, brief)\n    - Rationale (why this improves structure)\n    - Exact paths/areas involved\n    - Risk classification (Low/Medium)\n    - Dependencies (what must be true before action)\n    - Rollback plan (how to undo/revert lightly)\n    - Proposed outcome (what state will be improved)\n    - Estimated size/scope (files involved)\n\n  ### Advisory Only\n  - This is a cleanup *proposal generation* request.\n  - Do not perform any mutations.\n  - All cleanup proposals must become separate communicative requests when executed.\n\n  ### Deliverables\n  - A list of three proposed cleanup slices.\n  - Each slice with the structured fields above.\n  - No actual changes or moves.\n\n  ### Evidence\n  - Include snippets from inventory findings that justify each proposal.\n  - Link to specific interactive report filters or patterns observed.\n  - Provide file counts/size summaries per proposal.\n\n  ### Constraints\n  - Keep proposals orthogonal (do not overlap scopes).\n  - Prioritize lowest-risk first.\n  - Do not assume missing context; refer to documented inventory results.\n\nformat_notes:\n  - Paste the block above into the INPUT PAD.\n  - Then run `communicate` to produce the proposals.",
      "codex_ack_plan": "Review reports/inventory dataset and produce three orthogonal low-risk cleanup proposals with evidence, bounded paths, risk/dependencies/rollback/scope estimates; proposal-only, no mutations.",
      "execution_log": "- 2026-02-13T05:16:50+00:00 Started low-risk cleanup proposal generation from inventory report signals (entropy hotspots, derived artifact density, root doc sprawl).\n- 2026-02-13T05:17:16+00:00 Extracted inventory evidence (findings, category sizes/counts, duplicate heuristics, dense subtrees) and drafted three orthogonal low-risk proposal slices with rollback/dependency boundaries.",
      "outputs": "Top 3 Lowest-Risk Cleanup Proposals (Advisory Only, No Mutations)\n\n1) Slice Title\n- Root-Level Status/Audit Doc Entry-Point Consolidation\n\nRationale\n- Inventory flags high root-level status/audit sprawl, which creates ambiguous \u201clatest source of truth\u201d signals with low technical risk to correct via documentation-layer consolidation.\n\nExact paths/areas involved\n- Root markdown status/audit set:\n  - `HANDOFF.md`\n  - `LATEST_UPDATE.md`\n  - `PHASE_D_VERIFICATION.md`\n  - `SESSION_HANDOFF_2026-02-13.md`\n  - `TECHNICAL_AUDIT.md`\n- Consolidation target area (proposal): `development_docs/` or `docs/` with one canonical root pointer file.\n\nRisk classification\n- Low\n\nDependencies\n- Agree canonical \u201ccurrent status\u201d artifact naming convention.\n- Decide archive location for superseded root status docs.\n\nRollback plan\n- Keep originals unchanged; if consolidation is unsatisfactory, revert pointer/index changes only.\n\nProposed outcome\n- Single authoritative entry point for status/audit navigation; reduced root clutter and less operator ambiguity.\n\nEstimated size/scope\n- ~5 files, ~55.9 KB total (root status/audit subset).\n\n2) Slice Title\n- Derived Artifact Boundary Normalization (Logs/Test Outputs)\n\nRationale\n- Inventory shows large derived footprint; low-risk cleanup is to standardize retention/placement boundaries without touching runtime code.\n\nExact paths/areas involved\n- `logs/` (60 files, ~127.3 MB)\n- `test_results/` (118 files, ~5.6 MB)\n- Subtrees with concentration:\n  - `logs/archive/`\n  - `test_results/test_audit/`\n  - `test_results/archive/`\n\nRisk classification\n- Low to Medium (operational)\n\nDependencies\n- Define retention windows and archive policy for generated artifacts.\n- Confirm what must remain committed for audit/compliance evidence.\n\nRollback plan\n- Non-destructive approach: archive moves only; rollback by restoring previous paths from VCS.\n\nProposed outcome\n- Clear authoritative vs derived boundary, lower noise in active working areas, improved scan/read performance for humans.\n\nEstimated size/scope\n- ~178 files, ~132.9 MB combined derived artifacts in `logs/` + `test_results/`.\n\n3) Slice Title\n- Duplicate-Named Report/Test Output Convention Pass\n\nRationale\n- Duplicate/near-duplicate naming heuristics indicate repeated report patterns (`summary`, `coverage`, `pytest_stdout/stderr`) across multiple trees; low-risk win is naming/location convention alignment.\n\nExact paths/areas involved\n- Heuristic duplicate groups seen in:\n  - `test_results/**` (e.g., repeated `pytest_stdout.txt`, `pytest_stderr.txt`, `coverage.json`)\n  - `scripts/test_results/latest_summary.txt`\n  - `development_docs/misc/**/latest_summary.txt`\n- Candidate normalization domains:\n  - summary artifact naming\n  - coverage artifact naming\n  - per-run output folder conventions\n\nRisk classification\n- Low to Medium\n\nDependencies\n- Choose canonical naming schema per artifact class.\n- Confirm whether historical artifact names must remain immutable for prior references.\n\nRollback plan\n- Perform convention changes incrementally by new-run output naming first; revert naming aliases if downstream consumers break.\n\nProposed outcome\n- Faster artifact discovery, lower ambiguity in audits, fewer \u201clatest\u201d/duplicate-name collisions.\n\nEstimated size/scope\n- 37 duplicate-pattern candidate groups detected; initial low-risk pass can target top 5 groups only.\n\nOrthogonality check\n- Slice 1: root documentation entry points.\n- Slice 2: generated operational/test artifact boundary management.\n- Slice 3: naming conventions within repeated generated/report outputs.\n- Scopes are distinct and can be executed independently.",
      "evidence": "Inventory findings used\n- High: Root-level status/audit document sprawl (5 matching root markdown files).\n- Medium: Large generated artifact footprint (178 files in logs/test_results).\n- Medium: Duplicate/near-duplicate naming patterns (37 candidates).\n\nSupporting counts/sizes extracted from `reports/inventory/data.json`\n- Root status/audit subset:\n  - HANDOFF.md, LATEST_UPDATE.md, PHASE_D_VERIFICATION.md, SESSION_HANDOFF_2026-02-13.md, TECHNICAL_AUDIT.md\n  - Count: 5 files\n  - Size total: 57,213 bytes\n- logs/: 60 files, 127,339,025 bytes\n- test_results/: 118 files, 5,622,018 bytes\n- development_docs/: 194 files, 4,404,583 bytes\n- Duplicate candidate groups: 37 (top patterns include `summary`, `pytest_stdout`, `pytest_stderr`, `coverage`).\n\nInteractive report patterns referenced\n- Findings panel: root sprawl, generated-artifact footprint, duplicate naming candidates.\n- Filters/tables used conceptually for each proposal:\n  - category filter (Development Docs / Operational Artifacts / Test Artifacts)\n  - largest directories and dense directories tables\n  - duplication candidates table\n\nNo mutations performed\n- This response is proposal generation only; no cleanup actions were executed.",
