      "request_id": "REQ-20260213-102524",
      "status": "DONE",
      "author": "USER",
      "created_at": "2026-02-13T16:25:24+00:00",
      "last_updated_at": "2026-02-13T16:59:33+00:00",
      "request_text": "problem_type: prescriptive\n\nobjective: >\n  Expand the existing trade-limit and off-hours analysis request to include\n  evaluation of a potential \u201cQuick Trading Mode\u201d (threshold-based rapid execution)\n  as a configurable strategy option for future AI/ML integration.\n  This is exploratory and analytical only \u2014 no implementation.\n\ndirective_to_codex: |\n  This request has three analytical components.\n  No code modifications. Modeling and evaluation only.\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------\n  PART 1 \u2014 Trade Limit Utilization & Opportunity Cost Analysis\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n  Context:\n  - Daily trade limit exists.\n  - Max open positions limit exists.\n  - Concern: early trades exhaust limits and block higher-quality later trades.\n\n  Required:\n\n    1. Time-of-Day Performance Analysis\n       - P&L grouped by time buckets.\n       - P&L grouped by trade index (1st, 2nd, etc.).\n       - Win rate vs time-of-day.\n       - Drawdown vs time-of-day.\n\n    2. Limit Saturation Modeling\n       - % days limits reached.\n       - Average time limits reached.\n       - Opportunity signals missed after limits hit.\n       - Simulated P&L if limits were:\n           A) higher\n           B) staggered\n           C) dynamically adjusted by volatility\n           D) gated by signal strength\n\n    3. Risk Evaluation\n       - Do early trades statistically outperform?\n       - Does reserving capacity reduce losses?\n\n    Output:\n       - Quantified summary tables.\n       - Charts.\n       - Recommendation: fixed vs dynamic policy.\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------\n  PART 2 \u2014 Quick Trading Mode (Exploratory Strategy Option)\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n  Hypothesis:\n  Introduce a configurable \u201cQuick Trading Mode\u201d that:\n    - Executes trades automatically once price gains or losses cross a\n      defined threshold.\n    - Captures micro-momentum.\n    - Maintains continuous compounding where possible.\n    - Would likely require AI/ML signal validation in the future.\n\n  This is conceptual and modeling only.\n\n  Required:\n\n    1. Strategy Definition\n       - Define Quick Trading Mode parameters:\n           * threshold % gain trigger\n           * threshold % loss exit\n           * max holding duration\n           * capital allocation rules\n           * interaction with daily trade/max position limits\n\n    2. Behavioral Comparison\n       Compare:\n         A) Current discretionary/strategy model\n         B) Quick Trading Mode (threshold-based)\n         C) Hybrid model (AI-gated quick trading)\n\n    3. Backtest Simulation\n       - Model hypothetical quick-entry/quick-exit trades on historical data.\n       - Evaluate:\n           * turnover rate\n           * slippage sensitivity\n           * transaction cost impact\n           * volatility exposure\n           * drawdown\n           * Sharpe or risk-adjusted return proxy\n\n    4. Constraint Interaction\n       - Would quick trading exhaust daily limits rapidly?\n       - Would position limits require redesign?\n       - Would it require separate capital bucket?\n\n    5. Risk Classification\n       - Overtrading risk\n       - Whipsaw risk\n       - Transaction cost drag\n       - Liquidity constraints\n       - Latency dependency\n\n    Output:\n       - Structured comparison matrix.\n       - Quantified trade-offs.\n       - Recommendation:\n           * viable\n           * conditional\n           * not advisable under current constraints\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------\n  PART 3 \u2014 Off-Hours Market-Watch Expansion\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n  Evaluate the feasibility of building an off-hours analysis system\n  to complement live trading.\n\n  Required:\n\n    1. Use Cases\n       - Pre-market scanning\n       - After-hours earnings monitoring\n       - Strategy simulation\n       - Signal pre-qualification\n       - Risk scenario modeling\n\n    2. Architecture Sketch\n       - Read-only analysis layer\n       - Strategy sandbox\n       - Watchlist optimizer\n       - Signal ranking engine\n\n    3. Strategic Value\n       - Does preparation improve:\n           * entry quality?\n           * position sizing?\n           * risk control?\n       - Is it additive or redundant?\n\n    4. Scope & Complexity\n       - Small / Medium / Large classification\n       - Separate product vs module\n\n    Output:\n       - Feasibility assessment.\n       - Minimal viable version outline.\n\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n  Final Deliverable Requirements:\n\n    - Clear separation between verified statistical results and assumptions.\n    - Structured tables and comparison matrices.\n    - Charts where appropriate.\n    - Explicit recommendation section.\n    - Executive summary (1 page equivalent).\n    - No code changes.\n    - No system mutation.\n\nconstraints:\n  - Analytical modeling only.\n  - Use historical trade data where available.\n  - Flag data gaps explicitly.\n  - Do not overstate confidence.\n\nevaluation_goal:\n  Produce a structured output that can be critically evaluated\n  for strategic direction before any system modifications occur.",
      "codex_ack_plan": "Analysis-only workflow: (1) inventory available historical datasets (trades, prices, logs); (2) compute verified metrics for time-of-day, trade-index, saturation, and off-hours proxies; (3) run hypothetical quick-trading-mode simulations with explicit assumptions and sensitivity bands; (4) produce structured matrices/tables + recommendations + confidence qualifiers; (5) complete with no code changes.",
      "execution_log": "- 2026-02-13T16:59:08+00:00 # Executive Summary (analysis-only, no code changes)\n\nUsing available paper-trading logs, the main observed bottleneck is **max open positions** (20), not daily trade limit (50).\n\nVerified findings:\n- No observed daily-limit hits in available trade window.\n- Risk rejections are dominated by `max open positions reached (20)` (355 events).\n- First open-position saturation appears at ~09:33 ET on 2026-02-13; 354 additional rejections follow that first hit.\n- Actionable signal volume on 2026-02-13 is high (370), implying capacity exhaustion risk for any higher-turnover mode.\n\nStrategic recommendation:\n- Trade-limit policy should remain fixed for now, but with **dynamic reserve logic tied to open-position utilization**, not only daily count.\n- \u201cQuick Trading Mode\u201d is **conditional at best** and **not advisable under current constraints** unless:\n  1) separate capital/risk bucket, 2) independent sub-limits, 3) stricter entry quality gate, 4) cost/slippage-aware execution layer.\n- Off-hours system is feasible and likely additive as a **read-only preparation layer**.\n\n---\n\n# Data Scope and Confidence\n\n## Verified dataset coverage\n- `logs/paper/trades.jsonl`: 136 orders across 2026-02-02 to 2026-02-13.\n- `logs/paper/equity.jsonl`: 11,013 equity snapshots.\n- `logs/paper/system/agent_events.jsonl`: active event stream used for risk/signal split.\n- Config snapshot from `data/paper/config_state.json`:\n  - `max_daily_trades=50`\n  - `max_open_positions=20`\n\n## Confidence qualifiers\n- Trade P&L estimates are **realized-round-trip approximations** from execution logs (average-cost reconstruction).\n- Signal/risk saturation metrics come from currently active event logs (strong for current day, weaker for longer historical continuity due log rotation/segmentation).\n\n---\n\n# PART 1: Trade Limit Utilization and Opportunity Cost\n\n## 1) Time-of-day performance (verified from reconstructed round trips, n=37)\n\n| Close Hour (ET) | n | Total P&L | Win Rate | Avg Return % | Max Realized DD* |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 09:00 | 11 | -191.90 | 18.2% | 14.271% | -937.75 |\n| 10:00 | 6  | -710.54 | 0.0%  | -2.165% | -1133.58 |\n| 11:00 | 10 | -495.08 | 0.0%  | -1.806% | -1223.75 |\n| 12:00 | 6  | +554.30 | 16.7% | 7.600% | -891.89 |\n| 13:00 | 3  | -35.87  | 0.0%  | -1.095% | -378.49 |\n| 15:00 | 1  | -86.47  | 0.0%  | -2.067% | -724.46 |\n\n*DD is drawdown on cumulative realized-P&L trajectory, grouped by close-hour.\n\nASCII view (P&L by close hour):\n- 09: -191.9  ####\n- 10: -710.5  ###############\n- 11: -495.1  ###########\n- 12: +554.3  ++++++++++++\n- 13: -35.9   #\n- 15: -86.5   ##\n\n## 2) P&L by trade index within day (verified)\n\n| Trade Index | n | Avg P&L | Win Rate |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 7 | -30.25 | 28.6% |\n| 2 | 4 | -77.43 | 0.0% |\n| 3 | 4 | -57.37 | 0.0% |\n| 4 | 4 | -69.80 | 0.0% |\n| 5 | 4 | -36.83 | 0.0% |\n| 6 | 4 | +119.17 | 25.0% |\n| 7 | 4 | -26.44 | 0.0% |\n| 8 | 3 | -20.22 | 0.0% |\n| 9 | 3 | -32.80 | 0.0% |\n\nInterpretation:\n- No strong evidence that earliest trades are superior in this sample.\n- Mid-sequence (index 6) was best, but sample is small.\n\n## 3) Saturation modeling (verified + modeled)\n\n### Verified saturation\n- Days with trades: 10\n- Days hitting daily trade limit (50): 0 (0.0%)\n- RiskCheckFailed reasons:\n  - `max open positions reached (20)`: 355\n- Open-position saturation day count in active event log: 1 day (2026-02-13)\n  - First hit ~09:33 ET\n  - Additional rejections after first hit: 354\n- Actionable signals after first open-limit hit (same day): 366 (missed-opportunity proxy)\n\n### Policy simulations (counterfactual on observed order stream)\nBaseline simulated realized P&L: +6.73 (blocked orders 62)\n\n| Scenario | Realized P&L | Blocked Orders | Delta vs Baseline |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Higher limits (+5 daily, +2 open) | -288.59 | 53 | -295.32 |\n| Staggered reserve (keep last 2 daily slots) | +6.73 | 62 | 0.00 |\n| Dynamic daily-cap proxy (tighter early window) | +6.73 | 62 | 0.00 |\n| Signal-gated proxy (higher-notional-only buys) | -1423.13 | 65 | -1429.86 |\n\nInterpretation:\n- In this sample, relaxing limits did **not** improve outcomes.\n- Open-position pressure dominates observed opportunity cost.\n\n## 4) Risk evaluation answer\n\nQuestion: Do early trades statistically outperform?\n- **No clear support** in available sample.\n\nQuestion: Does reserving capacity reduce losses?\n- **No measurable benefit** in this sample under simple reserve proxies; effect likely policy-definition sensitive.\n\nRecommendation (Part 1):\n- Prefer **dynamic policy around open-position utilization** over merely increasing fixed limits.\n- Keep daily cap fixed initially; add reserve logic only if paired with entry-quality gating and validation.\n\n---\n\n# PART 2: Quick Trading Mode (Exploratory)\n\n## 1) Candidate parameter definition (conceptual)\n\nProposed parameter surface:\n- `gain_take_pct`: 0.5% to 1.5%\n- `loss_cut_pct`: 0.5% to 1.5%\n- `max_hold_minutes`: 5 to 60\n- `capital_bucket_pct`: 5% to 20% (isolated from core strategy)\n- `max_quick_trades_per_day`: separate from global daily cap\n- `max_quick_positions`: sub-cap below global open-position limit\n\n## 2) Behavioral comparison matrix\n\n| Model | Strength | Primary Risk | Constraint Fit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Current strategy flow | Lower churn, clearer risk envelope | Opportunity latency | Better fit with existing caps |\n| Quick Trading Mode | Higher responsiveness, momentum capture potential | Overtrading, whipsaw, fee/slippage drag | Poor fit unless separate limits/bucket |\n| Hybrid AI-gated quick mode | Potentially better selectivity | Model risk + complexity + latency coupling | Conditional fit with strong controls |\n\n## 3) Backtest-style quick-mode modeling (assumption-bounded)\n\nAssumption: without intrabar path, realized trade return is truncated to threshold band as proxy.\n\n| Threshold | n | Avg Return % | Return Vol % | Sharpe Proxy | Sum Return % |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| \u00b10.5% | 37 | -0.419 | 0.277 | -24.032 | -15.500 |\n| \u00b11.0% | 37 | -0.838 | 0.553 | -24.032 | -31.000 |\n| \u00b11.5% | 37 | -1.173 | 0.822 | -22.655 | -43.390 |\n\nCaveat: this is scenario modeling, not true intraday path backtest.\n\n## 4) Constraint interaction analysis\n\nVerified interaction pressure:\n- On 2026-02-13, actionable signals = 370.\n- If quick mode took only 20% of actionable signals as round trips:\n  - ~74 round trips/day => ~148 orders/day (entry+exit), exceeding daily cap 50.\n- Therefore quick mode likely requires:\n  - separate quick-mode cap,\n  - separate capital bucket,\n  - open-position sub-cap,\n  - stronger pre-entry quality filters.\n\n## 5) Risk classification\n\n- Overtrading risk: High\n- Whipsaw risk: High\n- Transaction cost drag: High sensitivity\n- Liquidity constraints: Medium/High (symbol dependent)\n- Latency dependency: High (execution timing critical)\n\nPart 2 recommendation:\n- **Conditional / not advisable under current constraints**.\n- Reassess only after dedicated risk/capacity partitioning and higher-fidelity intraday backtest data.\n\n---\n\n# PART 3: Off-Hours Market-Watch Expansion\n\n## 1) Feasibility and observed signal context\n\nVerified signal split from active event log:\n- Market-open signals: 3,730; actionable 360 (9.65%)\n- Market-closed signals: 15,503; actionable 2,387 (15.40%)\n\nInterpretation:\n- Off-hours pipeline is active and can provide substantial candidate signal flow for pre-qualification.\n\n## 2) Architecture sketch (analysis-layer only)\n\nRecommended components:\n1. Read-only analysis layer (no order privileges)\n2. Strategy sandbox (counterfactual scoring)\n3. Watchlist optimizer (ranking/prioritization)\n4. Signal ranking engine (confidence + risk-adjusted score)\n\n## 3) Strategic value\n\nLikely additive benefits:\n- Better pre-market entry quality\n- Better pre-position sizing planning\n- Earlier risk scenario visibility\n\nRisk of redundancy:\n- Low to moderate, provided it remains pre-trade analytical and not duplicate runtime execution logic.\n\n## 4) Scope and complexity\n\n- MVP scope: Medium\n- Full productization: Large\n- Recommended packaging: module first (inside current system), not separate product initially.\n\nMVP outline:\n1. Off-hours signal digest + rank report\n2. Next-session candidate queue with rationale\n3. Capacity-aware pre-market plan (limits + open-position budget)\n\n---\n\n# Verified vs Assumed Boundary\n\n## Verified\n- Trade/equity/event counts and timestamps from logs.\n- Config limits from paper config-state.\n- Risk failure reason distribution.\n- Observed open-position saturation behavior.\n\n## Assumed / Modeled\n- Quick-mode threshold simulations (path-truncation proxy due missing intrabar path).\n- Policy scenario effects under counterfactual limit logic.\n- Any Sharpe-like interpretation from small-sample reconstructed returns.\n\n---\n\n# Final Recommendation\n\n1. Keep current global daily limit fixed for now; do not increase by default.\n2. Prioritize open-position saturation controls (capacity budgeting + selective entry) over daily-cap expansion.\n3. Treat Quick Trading Mode as conditional R&D only; not production-viable under current constraint regime.\n4. Build off-hours analysis MVP as a read-only prep layer; use it to improve next-session prioritization and limit-aware planning.\n5. Before any implementation, collect higher-fidelity intraday price path and slippage/cost data for true quick-mode backtesting.\n\nNo code changes and no system mutation were performed for this request.",
      "outputs": "Completed full analysis-only report across trade-limit utilization, quick-trading-mode exploratory modeling, and off-hours expansion feasibility with verified-vs-assumed separation and recommendations.",
      "evidence": "Data used: logs/paper/trades.jsonl (136 orders), logs/paper/equity.jsonl (11013 points), logs/paper/system/agent_events.jsonl (risk/signal events), data/paper/config_state.json (limits: daily=50, open=20). Key verified finding: 355 RiskCheckFailed events due to max open positions; 0 observed daily-limit hits in available window.",
      "next_steps_if_blocked": "",
      "objective": "problem_type: prescriptive"
    },
