      "request_text": "codex> Generate best practice responses to identified roadmap weaknesses\n\nProblem Type:\ndescriptive + prescriptive\n\nObjective:\nFor each of the lower-scoring dimensions identified in the Strategic Roadmap Assessment (quantification & evaluability, operational discipline, productization readiness), produce best-practice recommendations that directly address the concerns and improve the maturity score. The output should be actionable and specific.\n\nMetric / Evaluative Standard:\n- Recommendations should be tied to a measurable improvement in the scored area.\n- Each recommendation must include:\n  - What action to take\n  - Why it improves the maturity score\n  - How to measure success\n  - Suggested milestones or checkpoints\n- Provide a short rationale for how each recommendation mitigates the identified gap.\n\nTime Horizon:\nImmediate strategic guidance\n\nScope:\n- Quantification & Evaluability (lowest score)\n- Operational Discipline\n- Productization Readiness\n\nConstraints:\nBinding:\n- Must not alter the core strategic direction of the document.\n- Must align with infrastructure governance context.\n- Must be actionable and not abstract.\n\nSoft:\n- Use industry best practices where applicable (e.g., SLI/SLO, KPI definition, versioning policies).\n\nIgnored:\n- Code or UI patch instructions (this is a strategic guidance document).\n\nDeliverables:\n\n1) **Quantification & Evaluability Recommendations**\n   - List of concrete metrics (e.g., MTTR, Mean Time to Deploy, Defect Escape Rate).\n   - For each metric:\n     - Definition\n     - Measurement method\n     - Target threshold\n     - Threshold for escalation\n\n2) **Operational Discipline Enhancements**\n   - Specific operational patterns to adopt (e.g., incident reviews, runbooks, test coverage gates).\n   - Recommended tooling integrations (e.g., log aggregators, alerting).\n\n3) **Productization Readiness Improvements**\n   - Concrete steps to define external contracts, compatibility matrices, versioning policies.\n   - Suggested packaging formats and distribution plans.\n\n4) **Verification Plan**\n   - How to validate that these recommendations improve maturity.\n   - Example checkpoints for each recommendation.\n\nExample commit message for codex output:",
      "codex_ack_plan": "Draft actionable best-practice recommendations for the three weak dimensions with measurable metrics, thresholds, milestones, and maturity-validation checkpoints; no code changes.",
      "execution_log": "- 2026-02-15T00:49:39+00:00 Preparing strategic response package aligned to roadmap governance context: quantification/evaluability, operational discipline, and productization readiness.",
      "outputs": "Quantification & Evaluability Recommendations\n\n1) Service Availability SLO (API health + core routes)\n- Action: Define SLI as successful requests / total requests for `/health`, `/api/health`, and top 3 user-critical API endpoints; adopt monthly SLO target.\n- Why it improves maturity: Converts \u201csystem seems stable\u201d into objective reliability targets with clear risk signals.\n- Measurement method: Aggregate nginx/app logs into request counters by route/status; calculate rolling 30-day success ratio.\n- Target threshold: >= 99.5% monthly success for critical endpoints.\n- Escalation threshold: < 99.0% in any rolling 7-day window.\n- Milestones:\n  - Week 1: baseline instrumentation and dashboard.\n  - Week 2: SLO definition ratified.\n  - Week 4: first monthly report with burn-rate analysis.\n- Gap mitigation rationale: Addresses missing quantifiable service health and decision-quality signals.\n\n2) MTTR (Mean Time to Recovery) for Sev-1/Sev-2 incidents\n- Action: Track incident start, detection, mitigation, and full recovery timestamps; compute MTTR per severity.\n- Why it improves maturity: Operational resilience becomes measurable and improvable instead of anecdotal.\n- Measurement method: Incident tickets with mandatory timestamp fields; auto-calc in incident tracker.\n- Target threshold: Sev-1 MTTR <= 60 minutes; Sev-2 MTTR <= 4 hours.\n- Escalation threshold: 2 consecutive incidents breaching MTTR targets.\n- Milestones:\n  - Week 1: incident template updated.\n  - Week 3: first MTTR trend chart published.\n  - Quarter end: 20% MTTR reduction vs baseline.\n- Gap mitigation rationale: Directly strengthens quantifiable operational responsiveness.\n\n3) Change Failure Rate (CFR)\n- Action: Measure percentage of deployments requiring rollback/hotfix/incident response within 24 hours.\n- Why it improves maturity: Links delivery quality to release process discipline.\n- Measurement method: CI deploy logs + issue labels (`rollback`, `hotfix`, `incident-linked`).\n- Target threshold: CFR <= 15%.\n- Escalation threshold: CFR > 20% for 2 release cycles.\n- Milestones:\n  - Week 2: release tagging policy active.\n  - Month 1: CFR baseline.\n  - Quarter 1: enforce release gate if threshold breached.\n- Gap mitigation rationale: Converts quality risk into an actionable release KPI.\n\n4) Defect Escape Rate\n- Action: Track production defects per release compared with pre-release defects found in test/staging.\n- Why it improves maturity: Improves evaluability of QA effectiveness and test strategy.\n- Measurement method: Ticket workflow tags (`found_in=prod|staging|test`) and release linkage.\n- Target threshold: <= 10% of total detected defects found in production.\n- Escalation threshold: > 15% for two months.\n- Milestones:\n  - Week 2: tagging policy adopted.\n  - Month 1: initial escape-rate report.\n  - Month 2: top-3 recurring escape causes remediated.\n- Gap mitigation rationale: Introduces concrete feedback loop from prod defects back to test design.\n\n5) Deployment Lead Time (commit to production)\n- Action: Measure elapsed time from merge-to-main to production release for standard changes.\n- Why it improves maturity: Balances speed with control and surfaces process bottlenecks.\n- Measurement method: Git metadata + deployment timestamps from CI/CD.\n- Target threshold: P50 <= 1 day, P90 <= 3 days.\n- Escalation threshold: P90 > 5 days for two consecutive sprints.\n- Milestones:\n  - Week 1: data extraction script.\n  - Sprint 2: bottleneck review.\n  - Sprint 3: process adjustments validated.\n- Gap mitigation rationale: Quantifies delivery efficiency and governance friction.\n\nOperational Discipline Enhancements\n\n1) Formal Incident Review Cadence\n- Action: Require blameless post-incident reviews for Sev-1/2 within 5 business days.\n- Why it improves maturity: Institutionalizes learning and recurring-risk reduction.\n- Success measure: 100% PIR completion SLA; >= 80% action items closed within target date.\n- Checkpoints:\n  - Week 1: PIR template and owner assigned.\n  - Month 1: first PIR quality review.\n\n2) Runbook Completeness Standard\n- Action: Create runbooks for top 5 operational failure modes (startup failure, blocked runtime, auth outage, data feed failure, deployment rollback).\n- Why it improves maturity: Reduces response variance and onboarding dependency.\n- Success measure: 100% of top incidents map to runbook; on-call can execute without creator assistance.\n- Checkpoints:\n  - Week 2: first 3 runbooks.\n  - Week 4: tabletop drill and gaps closed.\n\n3) Release Readiness Gate (lightweight)\n- Action: Add pre-release checklist gate: health checks pass, rollback tested, migration impact documented, alerting active.\n- Why it improves maturity: Prevents known classes of avoidable production regressions.\n- Success measure: 0 releases without checklist; CFR trend down after 2 cycles.\n- Checkpoints:\n  - Sprint 1: checklist enforced in PR/deploy workflow.\n  - Sprint 2: audit for bypasses.\n\n4) Observability Minimum Bar\n- Action: Require structured logs (request_id, route, outcome, latency_ms, correlation_id), alert thresholds for 5xx rate and latency.\n- Why it improves maturity: Enables faster diagnosis and objective service governance.\n- Tooling integrations:\n  - Log aggregation: Loki/ELK/Cloud-native equivalent.\n  - Metrics/alerts: Prometheus + Alertmanager (or managed alternative).\n  - Uptime checks: external probe for `/health` and `/api/health`.\n- Success measure: >95% incident timelines reconstructed from telemetry without ad hoc logging changes.\n- Checkpoints:\n  - Month 1: dashboard + alert rules live.\n  - Month 2: false-positive tuning complete.\n\n5) Test and Contract Gates\n- Action: Enforce CI gates: unit/integration pass, minimum coverage floor, API contract checks for breaking responses.\n- Why it improves maturity: Improves deployment confidence and external stability.\n- Success measure: 100% release branches pass mandatory gates; contract-break incidents reduced to near-zero.\n- Checkpoints:\n  - Sprint 1: coverage floor + failing gate enabled.\n  - Sprint 2: contract tests added for top endpoints.\n\nProductization Readiness Improvements\n\n1) External Contract Definition\n- Action: Publish a versioned API contract (OpenAPI + explicit error schemas + auth behavior) and consumer-facing compatibility policy.\n- Why it improves maturity: Converts internal behavior into dependable external interface commitments.\n- Success measure: All externally used endpoints documented with request/response examples and deprecation rules.\n- Milestones:\n  - Month 1: v1 contract baseline.\n  - Month 2: changelog + deprecation notice policy.\n\n2) Versioning Policy (SemVer + support window)\n- Action: Adopt SemVer for API/runtime artifacts and document support windows (e.g., N and N-1 minor versions).\n- Why it improves maturity: Enables predictable upgrades for integrators and internal ops.\n- Success measure: 100% releases tagged/versioned with change category (breaking/feature/fix).\n- Milestones:\n  - Sprint 1: release template updated.\n  - Sprint 2: automated release-note generation from labels.\n\n3) Compatibility Matrix\n- Action: Define and publish tested compatibility across OS/runtime/dependencies (Python versions, nginx baseline, broker API constraints).\n- Why it improves maturity: Reduces environment drift and integration failures.\n- Success measure: Every release validated against matrix; failures tracked and resolved before GA.\n- Milestones:\n  - Month 1: matrix v1 published.\n  - Quarter 1: automated matrix smoke tests.\n\n4) Packaging & Distribution Plan\n- Action: Standardize deliverables (e.g., signed release tarball + deployment manifest + checksum) and optional container image path for standardized environments.\n- Why it improves maturity: Makes deployments repeatable and auditable.\n- Success measure: Reproducible deployment from artifact bundle in <= 30 minutes by non-author operator.\n- Milestones:\n  - Sprint 2: artifact schema + checksum policy.\n  - Sprint 3: dry-run installation in clean host.\n\n5) Consumer-Facing Operational SLA Documentation\n- Action: Document expected service behavior, known limits, maintenance windows, and incident communication protocol.\n- Why it improves maturity: Aligns stakeholder expectations and reduces ad hoc support burden.\n- Success measure: Stakeholder onboarding checklist completion and reduced support ambiguity tickets.\n- Milestones:\n  - Month 1: SLA/operations page published.\n  - Month 2: feedback loop integrated.\n\nVerification Plan\n\n1) Maturity Scorecard (monthly)\n- Build a three-dimension scorecard with weighted sub-metrics:\n  - Quantification & Evaluability: SLO adherence, MTTR trend, CFR, defect escape.\n  - Operational Discipline: PIR completion, runbook coverage, release gate compliance.\n  - Productization Readiness: contract completeness, versioning compliance, compatibility pass rate.\n- Validation criterion: each dimension improves by >= 1 maturity level or >= 20% composite score in 2 quarters.\n\n2) Checkpoint cadence\n- 30-day checkpoint:\n  - Baselines captured for all core metrics.\n  - Incident/release templates and runbook standards active.\n- 60-day checkpoint:\n  - First trend improvements visible (MTTR/CFR/escape rate).\n  - API contract v1 + version policy published.\n- 90-day checkpoint:\n  - Gate compliance >95%.\n  - Compatibility matrix active and tested.\n  - Scorecard indicates sustained positive trend.\n\n3) Escalation governance\n- If 2 consecutive checkpoints miss targets:\n  - Trigger roadmap corrective action review.\n  - Freeze non-critical feature work for one sprint to close operational debt.\n\nShort rationale across all recommendations\n- These actions preserve current strategic direction but add measurable control points, explicit ownership, and contract discipline. That combination directly closes the identified maturity gaps by making reliability, operations, and product readiness auditable rather than qualitative.",
      "evidence": "No code changes performed.\nDeliverable is strategic guidance aligned to the existing roadmap direction and governance context.\nOutput includes measurable metrics, targets/escalation thresholds, milestones, and a verification cadence mapped to the three low-scoring dimensions.",
      "next_steps_if_blocked": "",
      "objective": "codex> Generate best practice responses to identified roadmap weaknesses"
    },
