      "request_text": "THE FOLLOWING IS A DISCUSSION BETWEEN ME AND ANOTHER CHATGPT REGARDING THE PLACEMENT OF THE '>' CHARACTER IN COMMANDS:\n\nME: > boost for the prompt engineering project prefixes as well as the '> communicate' command (which implementation was set by codex)... what is the most logical, efficient, textbook (synonym meaning like common knowledge or current format or something...) way to label the prefixes. I think codex was trying to go '> communicate' to mimic Linux commands. I think this is confusing using our commands and if I need to add something to communicate as well (should never happen but nev er know, could be a test...) but > <command_name> <text> ie > boost all channels to redline there could be discrepancy vs. boost> (improve what follows the '>') right? ensure it works for 'communicate' for codex, why did it switch it everytime I used it\n\nChatGPT: You\u2019re identifying a real ambiguity. You currently have two competing grammars:\n\nboost> (suffix operator style)\n\n> communicate (shell-style command)\n\nThey parse differently and create routing confusion.\n\nRoot Problem\n\nYou have mixed two syntactic models:\n\nModel A \u2014 Functional Prefix Operator\nboost> text\ndra> text\nse_agent> text\n\n\nSemantics:\n\noperator> payload\n\nOperator transforms what follows.\n\nLeft \u2192 right chaining works cleanly.\n\nThis matches your structured prefix pipeline.\n\nModel B \u2014 Shell Command Style\n> communicate text\n> boost text\n\n\nSemantics:\n\n> command args\n\nLooks like Linux.\n\nBut conflicts with Model A.\n\nBreaks mental mapping of \u201cprefix modifies what follows.\u201d\n\nYou cannot safely support both without ambiguity.\n\nWhy Codex \u201cswitched it\u201d\n\nBecause:\n\n> communicate visually resembles CLI.\n\nboost> resembles a redirection or custom operator.\n\nLLMs tend to normalize toward common shell grammar when they see > at line start.\n\nThere is no single enforced grammar, so it drifted.\n\nThis is structural, not accidental.\n\nTextbook / Cleanest Solution\n\nPick one grammar class and eliminate the other.\n\nThe most logical and scalable for your architecture is:\n\nAdopt Operator Style Everywhere\nCanonical Form\nboost> dra> se_agent> payload\ncommunicate> payload\n\nRules\n\nNo leading > ever.\n\nAlways <command_name> >\n\nLeft \u2192 right transformation.\n\nFinal stage = executor.\n\nNo space before >.\n\nThis keeps:\n\nDeterministic parsing\n\nVisual symmetry\n\nChaining clarity\n\nNo collision with shell syntax\n\nNo confusion between payload and modifier\n\nWhy This Is Superior\nCriterion\tOperator Style (boost>)\tShell Style (> boost)\nSupports chaining\tYes\tClumsy\nMatches your pipeline model\tYes\tNo\nAmbiguity risk\tLow\tHigh\nVisual clarity\tHigh\tMedium\nExtendable\tYes\tLimited\nClean Code SRP alignment\tYes\tMixed\n\nYour architecture is functional composition, not CLI invocation. So use functional composition syntax.\n\nFix for communicate\n\nMake it:\n\ncommunicate> text\n\n\nSemantics:\n\nSingle-stage executor instruction\n\nNo Linux mimicry\n\nTreated like any other prefix\n\nCan be chained if ever required:\n\nboost> communicate> text\n\n\nEven if that\u2019s rare, the grammar stays consistent.\n\nDisallow This Completely\n> communicate\n> boost\n> anything\n\n\nIt introduces:\n\nDual parsing paths\n\nAmbiguous routing\n\nHidden transformation expectations\n\nFormal Naming Convention (Most Logical Labeling)\n\nYou currently call them \u201cprefixes.\u201d That\u2019s accurate but incomplete.\n\nMore precise, textbook-aligned labels:\n\nOperators (best choice)\n\nPipeline Operators\n\nTransformation Operators\n\nExecution Operator (for communicate)\n\nRecommended terminology:\n\nPipeline Operators\n\nBecause:\n\nThey are evaluated left \u2192 right.\n\nThey transform payload.\n\nExecution must be final.\n\nIt matches compiler and functional programming models.\n\nClean Spec Recommendation\n\nGrammar:\n\n<operator_name> > <payload>\n\n\nChaining:\n\n<operator> > <operator> > <payload>\n\n\nConstraints:\n\nNo leading >\n\nNo spaces inside operator token\n\nExecution operator must be final\n\nPayload must be explicit\n\nDirect Answer to Your Discrepancy Concern\n\nYes.\n\n> boost all channels to redline\n\nand\n\nboost> all channels to redline\n\nare semantically different grammars.\n\nIf both are allowed, discrepancy is guaranteed.\n\nFinal Recommendation\n\nStandardize to operator> form only.\n\nRename \u201cprefix\u201d to \u201cPipeline Operator.\u201d\n\nConvert > communicate to communicate>.\n\nExplicitly forbid leading > syntax.\n\nDocument grammar once and freeze it.\n\nThat resolves:\n\nDrift\n\nCodex normalization\n\nFuture extension conflicts\n\nAmbiguity between modifier and payload\n\nNothing else needs to change architecturally.",
      "codex_ack_plan": "Review command grammar and current runtime docs, reconcile with existing command-word contract, and provide a deterministic recommendation for command syntax standardization (including communicate behavior) with explicit do/don't rules.",
      "execution_log": "- 2026-02-18T02:22:02+00:00 Starting grammar analysis for operator-style vs leading-gt command style and impact on commscribe command-word behavior.\n- 2026-02-18T02:22:21+00:00 Completed syntax reconciliation: compared operator-style vs leading-gt command style against current repo contracts and identified mixed/incorrect command token drift in runtime docs.",
      "outputs": "Structured Decision\n- Adopt one grammar only: operator suffix style (`command>`).\n- Canonical execution trigger: `communicate>`.\n- Canonical prompt operators: `boost>`, `dra>`, `se_agent>`.\n\nGrammar Contract\n- Allowed:\n  - `communicate>`\n  - `boost> <payload>`\n  - `dra> <payload>`\n  - `se_agent> <payload>`\n  - chained operator style only: `boost> dra> se_agent> <payload>`\n- Disallowed:\n  - `> communicate`\n  - `> boost`\n  - mixed shell-style + operator-style in same system\n\nWhy This Is Correct (deterministic criteria)\n- Single parser model (operator transforms payload left-to-right).\n- No shell-grammar collision.\n- Supports chaining without ambiguity.\n- Matches existing commscribe behavior trigger usage (`communicate>`).\n\nCurrent Contract Drift Detected\n- Mixed token forms currently present in runtime docs (e.g., `communicate>_scan`, leading-`>` command forms).\n- This is a documentation/contract drift issue, not a parser capability requirement.\n\nCorrected Canonical Token Set (for future standardization)\n- `communicate>`\n- `communicate_scan` (internal script/command name, not operator form)\n- `governance_check` (internal command name)\n- `startup_check` (internal command name)\n\nOperational Rule\n- In conversation/operator layer: use only `command>` for operator triggers.\n- In shell/runtime layer: use actual command names without synthetic operator mutation.",
      "evidence": "Sources Reviewed\n- ai_runtime/PROJECT_RUNTIME.md\n- ai_runtime/STARTUP_PROTOCOL.md\n- commscribe/PROJECT_RUNTIME.md\n- commscribe/CODEX_COMMUNICATE_INSTRUCTIONS.md\n- commscribe/README.md\n- README.md\n\nValidation Checks\n- Located and compared current command-word declarations.\n- Confirmed mixed syntax drift exists in repo docs.\n- Confirmed `communicate>` semantics are explicitly documented as command-word trigger behavior.\n\nNo-Change Compliance\n- No files modified for this request.",
